{"id": "48:12", "severity": "major", "issues": [{"latin": "vocavérunt nómina sua in terris suis", "portuguese": "chamaram as suas terras pelos seus nomes", "problem": "Restructures the Latin: in the Latin, nomina sua is the object and in terris suis is a place ('in/upon their lands'). The Portuguese makes 'terras' the object and adds 'pelos', which is the Douay/Hebrew-derived explanation (they named their estates after themselves). The Latin keeps it open: they called (invoked, proclaimed) their names in their lands.", "fix": "chamaram os seus nomes nas suas terras"}]},
{"id": "48:15b", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "in inférno a glória eórum", "portuguese": "no inferno, longe da sua glória", "problem": "'longe da' decides what 'a' means (separation, 'far from'); the Latin 'a gloria eorum' is ambiguous (from / away from / on the part of their glory). 'longe' adds a nuance of distance not in the Latin.", "fix": "no inferno, desde a sua glória"}]},
{"id": "48:18", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "non sumet ómnia", "portuguese": "nada levará", "problem": "The Latin says 'he will not take all things'; 'nada' resolves it to 'nothing' (the Hebraism behind the LXX), which the Latin wording does not itself state.", "fix": "não levará todas as coisas"}]}
],
"overall": "A faithful, close rendering that keeps the psalm's Septuagintal hard spots as they stand (the dangling 'E o preço do resgate' in v9, 'o homem resgatará' without a question in v8, 'a iniquidade do meu calcanhar', 'acharão prazer na sua boca'). The one real fault is v12b, where the Douay-style explanation 'chamaram as suas terras pelos seus nomes' replaces the Latin's syntax; v15b and v18 are small resolutions of ambiguity. In v19 'te/fizeres' continues the 'tu' of v17 rather than taking 'tibi' as God; the Latin's second singular is consistent with that and I do not flag it. Pointing marks match the Latin in every verse."}
